<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izz al-Din Shir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1423">1423</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izz al-Din Shir was a Kurdish emir of Hakkari in the late 14th century, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julamerk">Julamerk</a> in the mountains of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s armies came through the region he submitted to the conqueror and was allowed to keep his lands. His family went on to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Hakkari for centuries, under Ottoman suzerainty from the 16th century until its abolition in the 1840s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Izz al-Din Shir, also called Yezdanşêr, is regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Hakkâri, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>. Sources place him in the line of Senek'erim, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senekerim-hovhannes-artsruni">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni</a>, the last ruler of the Kingdom of Vaspurakan. The descent was probably through his mother or grandmother, since marriages between Armenian Christian nobles and Kurdish Muslim ruling families were not unusual; alternatively he may have belonged to an Islamized branch of the Artsrunid family. His father may have been a Malik Izz al-Din who commissioned the Halima Khatun Mausoleum in Vostan in 1335.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The year his rule began is uncertain. In 1384 he appears as an ally of Sultan Ahmad of the Jalairid Sultanate, whom he led to safety through his lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> while Qara Mahammad of the Qara Qoyunlu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> threatened him. In 1387 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s forces besieged him at a fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushpa">Tushpa</a>; he was captured and bound, and many of the defenders were reportedly enslaved or killed, but he was spared and restored under Timurid suzerainty. In 1392 he took Vostan from his brother Malik Assad, and the next year the Catholicos of Aghtamar, Zak'aria II, was stoned to death on his orders.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s ally he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> of the Qara Qoyunlu in 1395, earning lands as a reward, and supported Timur's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>. In 1398 Qara Yusuf captured him, imprisoned him on Akdamar Island and plundered his lands, including the fortress of Diz and his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julamerk">Julamerk</a>. By Timur's death in 1405 he had gone over to Qara Yusuf, and he once acted as intermediary in a dispute between the Turkomans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1412, because of his age, he had begun handing power to his son Malik Muhammad, though he seems to have remained ruler in name. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> reconquered the region in 1421, Izz al-Din Shir went to his winter quarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> to offer fealty. He refused to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-iskander">Qara Iskander</a> when he briefly regained the area, a policy his successors kept, and he died in 1423. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> he founded lasted until 1845.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Izz al-Din Shir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 14th century a dynasty based at Julamerk (Colemerik) emerged; its emir <b>Izz al-Din Shir</b> submitted to Timur and kept his lands, and his family ruled the region thereafter.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-hakkari">Emirate of Hakkari</a></li></ul></section>
